ebook?ref=killscreen.com","line":54},{"title":"wrote in 2023","target":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1257685?ref=killscreen.com","line":56},{"title":"has provided a helpful model","target":"https://www.killscreen.com/why-risa-punos-games-ask-you-to-be-vulnerable-in-public/","line":80},{"title":"argues that the false boundary between games and life forgoes","target":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/240286002_Digital_Games_and_Escapism?ref=killscreen.com","line":86},{"title":"Source","target":"https://www.binaura.net/stc/io/?ref=killscreen.com","line":90}],"metadata":{"title":"What Gaming Teaches Us About AI Companionship and Digital Relationships","source":"https://www.killscreen.com/what-gaming-tells-us-about-ai-companionship/","author":["[[Jamin Warren]]"],"published":"2025-07-29","created":"2026-05-15","description":"Gaming culture provides crucial context for understanding AI companionship fears. From coin-operated addiction loops to World of Warcraft's endless social worlds, games have navigated similar moral panics for decades. One user's relationship with AI Donatello reveals the need for \"emotional intelligence\" to balance multiple realities—seeing both authentic connection and algorithmic design.","tags":["#clippings"]},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[],"last_embed":{"hash":"fglai7","at":1780884513704},"key":"Clippings/What Gaming Teaches Us About AI Companionship and Digital Relationships.md"},
